WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.580
- [Narrator] This little chunk
of metal contains lithium.

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:04.893
So does this cell phone
and this electric car.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.160
The super light metal
is a critical component

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.160
in batteries and the world's
transition to green energy.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.680
It's ignited a global
lithium surge in demand

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:14.556
which is expected to reach

00:00:14.556 --> 00:00:18.360
nearly 2.5 million metric tons by 2028.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.870
- Lithium is the white
gold of the electric age.

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:22.703
- [Narrator] China has become

00:00:22.703 --> 00:00:24.256
the world's largest lithium processor,

00:00:24.256 --> 00:00:26.520
controlling 55% of the market.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.470
To fuel its lithium supply chain,

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:30.750
China needs to secure raw lithium abroad.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:33.510
It's making risky major
investments in lithium mines

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:35.910
in Africa and Latin America
in hopes of controlling

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:37.799
one third of the world's
raw lithium supply

00:00:37.799 --> 00:00:40.301
as it competes with
countries around the world

00:00:40.301 --> 00:00:42.780
to feed a growing demand
for lithium batteries.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.393
Here's why experts say
China is trying to protect

00:00:45.393 --> 00:00:47.598
and expand its raw lithium sources

00:00:47.598 --> 00:00:49.102
and how the US is struggling

00:00:49.102 --> 00:00:51.093
to develop its own supply chain.

00:00:52.192 --> 00:00:55.134
Before lithium ends up in all of these,

00:00:55.134 --> 00:00:58.920
it first has to be processed
in refineries like this one

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.220
which transforms lithium
compounds into battery components.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.553
China specializes as the world's
largest lithium processor,

00:01:05.553 --> 00:01:09.240
processing about 55% of
the world's raw lithium.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.790
After processing the
mineral, it stays in China

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:13.738
or could travel to Japan or South Korea

00:01:13.738 --> 00:01:15.857
to be turned into battery components.

00:01:15.857 --> 00:01:18.450
Those batteries are then
shipped around the world,

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:19.555
including the US.

00:01:19.555 --> 00:01:22.800
The US processes very
little lithium domestically,

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.110
and it's reliant on
China's lithium processing.

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:27.870
China, on the other hand,
made significant investments

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:30.870
in its domestic lithium
refineries in the 2000s.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.960
- China started doing it
because you need a lot of energy

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.450
for refining and you also need the stomach

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:40.110
'cause it's a little bit of
a sort of dirty thing to do.

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:42.990
China manufactured a
lot of these appliances,

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:45.780
just got interested in
it and got good in it.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.075
And then of course
there was also this push

00:01:48.075 --> 00:01:52.740
by the Chinese government
to electrify transportation

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:54.698
and scale up electric vehicles.

00:01:54.698 --> 00:01:57.030
- [Narrator] China currently
has the largest market

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:59.220
for electric cars, with around 60%

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:00.780
of global electric car sales.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.360
And those cars need lithium batteries,

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.480
fueling China's demand for a
reliable lithium supply chain.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:08.560
- Lithium is just incredibly valuable

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:12.684
because it's an essential
component in the technologies

00:02:12.684 --> 00:02:17.460
that we need in order to achieve
a lot of our climate goals

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:19.440
and to electrify the world.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.099
Now in China's case,

00:02:21.099 --> 00:02:24.000
it's just been a little
bit ahead of the curve.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.360
So for them, in order
to maintain their lead

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.030
and in order to actually
make the things that they do,

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:31.800
they need a supply of lithium.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.200
- [Narrator] But China's
supply chain has a weak link.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.035
Even though it's the
world's largest processor,

00:02:36.035 --> 00:02:39.450
it relies on foreign countries
for much of its raw lithium,

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:40.813
including US allies.

00:02:40.813 --> 00:02:44.280
Chinese companies also mine
some lithium domestically,

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:45.930
and they're fighting for control.

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:49.500
Global lithium prices were
about 300% higher in August

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.720
than they were at the beginning of 2021.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:54.960
- In China, as is the
case all over the world,

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:56.340
people discovered that lithium

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.452
is becoming a very important commodity.

00:02:58.452 --> 00:03:01.020
Demand for the commodity also shot up.

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:05.250
So when the local
government auctioned rights

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:08.430
to explore a mine, the
bidding war just started,

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:13.430
and they submitted thousands
of bids and the prices went up.

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:16.200
- [Narrator] One recent
auction for exploration rights

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:19.860
at a lithium site in Sichuan
received over 11,000 bids.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.170
And the auction for the Jiada Lithium Mine

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:24.672
closed at the equivalent of $580 million,

00:03:24.672 --> 00:03:27.532
1300 times higher than its starting price.

00:03:27.532 --> 00:03:30.090
Lithium is an abundant mineral globally,

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:32.652
but it's often difficult
and expensive to extract.

00:03:32.652 --> 00:03:35.280
- At the moment, China
actually doesn't mine

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:36.450
a lot of lithium.

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:38.790
Most of the mining of lithium takes place

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:41.051
in Latin America and Australia,

00:03:41.051 --> 00:03:45.132
which actually exposes Chinese companies

00:03:45.132 --> 00:03:49.449
to a supply chain vulnerability
of getting the raw material.

00:03:49.449 --> 00:03:51.600
Chinese companies are really worried

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.736
that a lot of the
countries that are friendly

00:03:53.736 --> 00:03:55.999
with the United States might cut off

00:03:55.999 --> 00:03:58.650
Chinese companies' abilities

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:02.400
to invest more in these countries.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.080
- [Narrator] Australia,
the world's largest

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.480
raw lithium supplier,
blocked the sale of a mine

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.540
to a company with a director
holding Chinese citizenship.

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:12.750
And Canada has similarly
ordered three Chinese companies

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:14.691
to sell their stakes in Canadian mines.

00:04:14.691 --> 00:04:18.060
Instead, China is turning
to countries in Africa

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:21.390
and Latin America for riskier
raw lithium investments.

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:23.827
In the past two years,
Chinese companies have spent

00:04:23.827 --> 00:04:27.630
$4.5 billion on stakes
in nearly 20 African

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:29.314
and Latin American lithium mines.

00:04:29.314 --> 00:04:32.400
- The strategy to go
to developing countries

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.490
is not without risks because
a lot of these countries

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:39.160
have somewhat unstable political regimes.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:42.360
They're quite underdeveloped

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:43.770
when it comes to infrastructure.

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:45.630
And if you wanna mine and do things,

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:50.160
you need roads, you need
power, you need facilities.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:51.965
- [Narrator] Many nations
are also tightening control

00:04:51.965 --> 00:04:53.289
over their mines.

00:04:53.289 --> 00:04:57.030
In December, Zimbabwe banned
the export of raw lithium,

00:04:57.030 --> 00:05:00.088
instead forcing companies to
process it within the country.

00:05:00.088 --> 00:05:01.740
- And then there's also something

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:03.240
called resource nationalism.

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:06.360
So a lot of these developing
countries say to themselves,

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:07.975
well, you know, we have these resources

00:05:07.975 --> 00:05:11.441
and selling it as a sort of raw product

00:05:11.441 --> 00:05:13.258
actually gets us less money

00:05:13.258 --> 00:05:16.500
than if we have a valued
added industry with that.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:18.575
So if we, for example, process lithium

00:05:18.575 --> 00:05:23.100
and maybe even one day
produce our own batteries

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:25.050
because that's actually where
you make a lot of the money

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:27.600
and it's also in a bit an opportunity

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.220
to upscale your labor force.

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:31.155
But that's obviously
easier said than done.

00:05:31.155 --> 00:05:32.880
- [Narrator] And in Latin America,

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:35.760
Mexico and Chile are
also tightening control.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:38.335
While Chile, Bolivia, and
Argentina are discussing

00:05:38.335 --> 00:05:40.170
creating a lithium cartel.

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:42.120
- A lot of these developing countries

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:43.380
come with their own risks.

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:45.052
Chinese companies are also concerned

00:05:45.052 --> 00:05:48.990
that US allied countries could limit

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:51.019
their access to lithium.

00:05:51.019 --> 00:05:53.498
- [Narrator] Some experts
disagree that China is vulnerable

00:05:53.498 --> 00:05:56.430
to US allies restricting their raw lithium

00:05:56.430 --> 00:05:58.950
and say that China's
investments in Latin America

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:00.750
and Africa are paving the path

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:02.880
for a stronger supply chain in the future.

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:05.694
- For a lot of these
countries that have lithium,

00:06:05.694 --> 00:06:08.220
China is actually a very good customer

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:11.220
because it has a steady
demand for lithium.

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:13.950
It doesn't just buy lithium
for lithium's sake, right?

00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:16.320
It buys it because it
needs it for product.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:18.840
So you know that they
will be there tomorrow

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:20.130
and the day after tomorrow,

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:22.410
and that gives them a lot of stability

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:25.315
in terms of the money, the
revenue that they can get.

00:06:25.315 --> 00:06:27.451
You may not get the highest price,

00:06:27.451 --> 00:06:29.610
but you get a very steady price,

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:31.620
and that can be very reassuring.

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:33.360
- [Narrator] Lithium
mining died out in the US

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:36.180
in the 20th century because
companies found cheaper methods

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:38.594
to harvest lithium in
South American salt lakes.

00:06:38.594 --> 00:06:40.414
- And because there was no mining,

00:06:40.414 --> 00:06:42.736
refining also left the US.

00:06:42.736 --> 00:06:45.771
- [Narrator] Now the US wants
to secure its own supply chain

00:06:45.771 --> 00:06:47.635
to reduce its reliance on China,

00:06:47.635 --> 00:06:49.530
but it has a long way to go.

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:51.510
Propelled by the Inflation Reduction Act

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:53.556
and a growing demand for
green energy sources,

00:06:53.556 --> 00:06:56.400
the US is also increasing its efforts

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:58.560
to tap into its own lithium deposits

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:00.638
and construct domestic lithium refineries.

00:07:00.638 --> 00:07:03.210
Tax credits and green energy subsidies

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:04.800
from the Inflation Reduction Act

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:07.314
discount the cost of
producing lithium by 10%,

00:07:07.314 --> 00:07:10.438
enticing companies to make
American lithium investments.

00:07:10.438 --> 00:07:13.260
Exxon Mobil plans on
entering the lithium market

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:14.594
by building one of the world's largest

00:07:14.594 --> 00:07:17.340
lithium processing facilities in Arkansas.

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:19.740
When completed, analysts
predict it could produce

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:23.520
75,000 to a 100,000 metric
tons of lithium a year.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:25.217
That would add up to 15% of all finished

00:07:25.217 --> 00:07:27.300
lithium product globally.

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:28.235
But there's a catch.

00:07:28.235 --> 00:07:30.419
Many of these lithium extraction plants

00:07:30.419 --> 00:07:32.100
haven't been built yet.

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:34.412
One upcoming mine owned
by Lithium Americas

00:07:34.412 --> 00:07:37.896
contains one of the largest
known lithium sources in the US.

00:07:37.896 --> 00:07:40.410
Lithium Americas told
the Wall Street Journal

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:42.840
that besides one other
small operational mine,

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:44.648
it has the only project in North America

00:07:44.648 --> 00:07:47.100
that's permitted and in construction.

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:50.155
Its mine, Thacker Pass, has
been in the works for 16 years

00:07:50.155 --> 00:07:52.454
and still has another
estimated three years

00:07:52.454 --> 00:07:53.970
of construction to go.

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:57.655
- It's an essential metal
for a lot of the technologies

00:07:57.655 --> 00:08:01.736
that we need for the
decarbonization of the world.

00:08:01.736 --> 00:08:05.850
Every country wants to get hold of lithium

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:08.853
so that they can build those technologies.

